keyInsights:
The strong Nullstellensatz implies the weak form: if $J$ is proper ($J 
eq k[\mathbf{x}]$), then $\sqrt{J} 
eq k[\mathbf{x}]$ (radicals of proper ideals are proper), so $I(V(J)) = \sqrt{J} 
eq k[\mathbf{x}]$, meaning $I(V(J))$ is not the zero ideal of the vanishing, so $V(J) 
eq \emptyset$.
